window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "lessons/absences.html", "title": "Absences & whole-class codes", "heads": ["One student out: pick the reason in Absence", "Whole class out: switch on Whole-class code and pick one", "Same code in other periods? Tick them", "Click Save, check the summary, and Save again", "Read the line it gives you"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Every day ⏱ About 3 minutes 🔁 Whenever someone is out You need: your students added Why: every school day in the service log needs either minutes or a reason code. A code tells the log why there was no service, and decides whether that day's minutes are still owed. 1 AB student absent · O field trip, assembly, or school function · S state assessment. The badge right after the student's name changes from PRESENT to the code. The card greys out (the accommodation chips, Activity, and the guide row), but Individual notes… stays open: type why, like ISS, day 1 . The note saves with the absent day and prints in column G of that day's service log line. There are no separate ISS, OSS, or BEST codes: code those days AB and put the reason in the note. 2 Use this when nobody in the class got service for the same reason. Every card takes the code, replacing any AB or O already picked. Code Means Lowers the minutes owed? SAB Staff absent No : the week may read short SUB Staff pulled to sub Yes, by that day's share ARD Staff at an ARD meeting Yes, by that day's share FLEX Staff flex day No : the week may read short S State assessment Yes, by a full period A student code ( AB , O , S ), on one card or the whole class, lowers what's owed by a full period (the bell schedule's length that day), never below 0. So a student out most of the week isn't flagged short because of your flex day. See Minutes report . 3 Under Also apply to: , tick each other period that had the same day (a flex day usually covers several). A period with nobody in it says no students and can't be ticked. Ticks are never remembered for the next day. 4 Because those periods aren't on screen, the app first says exactly what it will do in each one. Nothing is written until you confirm. 5 One line per period it saved, naming the code. ✓ You'll know it worked The badge after each name shows the code, and an absent card is greyed except its note. A line per period, like Saved 1 student for period 2, 2026-10-20, all coded FLEX. Those days drop off the Entry tab's Unresolved dates . ✕ Avoid these A note on a whole-class code. A whole-class code takes no notes; only AB , O , and S on a card keep one. SAB and FLEX for anything else. They keep the minutes owed, so use them only when it was really you out. Expecting it to undo itself. To take a whole-class code off, open that period on that date, switch it off, fill in the day, and Save. ⚠ If you see this Coding these students will remove their already-logged sessions … That day was already saved as present. Remove sessions and save replaces it with the code; Cancel leaves it. Choose a whole-class code before saving other periods. Other periods are ticked but no code is picked. Pick one, then Save. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/acc-report.html", "title": "The Accommodations report", "heads": ["Settings → Accommodations report", "Read the table", "Print it, or save it as a PDF"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Paste & report ⏱ About 4 minutes 🔁 Before an ARD You need: some logged days Why: it counts how often each accommodation was delivered or declined. For an \"upon request\" accommodation a student never uses, that count is the evidence for removing it at an ARD. 1 Pick the Period , optionally one Student in it (leave it on Whole class otherwise), the dates, then Compute . 2 For one student: every accommodation on their list, even ones never used, with Days present , Delivered , Delivered % of days present , Used when offered , Last delivered , and Declined with the dates. Never-used ones and the most-declined come first, so the strongest cases for an ARD are at the top. For the whole class: each accommodation's delivered and declined counts, with who declined it on which date. Used when offered is delivered out of delivered + declined: only the days you offered it count. An accommodation needed only some days (oral administration on test days, say) can read 40% of days present but 100% used when offered, like Chunked assignments here. 3 Print… sits beside Compute . It's greyed until Compute shows a result, and greys again if you change a choice (period, student, a date) until you Compute again, so the printout always matches its heading. Click it and the Windows print dialog opens: pick a printer, or Microsoft Print to PDF to save a PDF for an ARD folder. The printout is just the report, headed with its name, your choices, and when it was printed. Nothing is saved. ✓ You'll know it worked Rows with declines show in red; never-used ones in yellow. Each day counts once, by that day's latest save. ✕ Avoid these Marking everything delivered \"just in case.\" The report is only as honest as the chips. Forgetting the second click. A declined accommodation is two clicks (✕), not one. ⚠ If you see this Pick both a start and an end date. Fill in From and To, then Compute. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/accommodations.html", "title": "Edit a student’s accommodations", "heads": ["Click the pencil next to ACCOMMODATIONS", "Change the list", "Set Effective to the ARD date, then Save accommodations", "The card updates"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson When the IEP changes ⏱ About 3 minutes 🔁 After an ARD You need: the ARD date Why: when an ARD adds, removes, or rewords an accommodation, change the list on the card. Days already logged keep exactly what was marked on them. 1 The pop-up lists the student's accommodations, one per line. 2 ↑ and ↓ move a line, Remove takes one off, Add accommodation adds an empty line to type in. One accommodation per line. 3 Scroll down in the pop-up for Save accommodations . Days before the date keep the old list. 4 A green line confirms it, and the new chip appears, unmarked like every chip each day. ✓ You'll know it worked A green line: Accommodations saved, effective … The new list on the card from that date on. ✕ Avoid these A semicolon inside a line. It would split it into two accommodations. Dating it today. Use the ARD date. Expecting it to mark anything. Every chip still starts unmarked each day. ⚠ If you see this \"…\" has a semicolon in it, which would split it into two accommodations. Reword it, or put each on its own line. Nothing changed, so nothing was saved. The list matches what's already there. There's already a roster change for this student starting … Use that date or later. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/add-students.html", "title": "Add your students", "heads": ["Pick from your roster", "Click the student’s period tab", "Type part of their name, and pick them", "Check the start date, then Confirm add", "Repeat for each student", "Paste from your Google Sheet", "On the student's period tab, click Paste from Sheets…", "Copy the row in your Sheet, then paste it in the box", "Check the start date, then Add to Period …", "The card appears", "Already in the app? You get the change review"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Start here · Stop 5 of 6 ⏱ About a minute per student 🔁 Once per student You need: your roster in the Roster folder, or your roster Sheet open Stop 5 of 6 Why: the app only shows students you add, one at a time, on the period tab they belong to. It takes their minutes, accommodations, and goal from your roster, so there's nothing to retype. There are two ways in: pick from the roster CSV (Part 1), or paste the student's row straight from your Google Sheet (Part 2), handy for a new arrival when you haven't downloaded the CSV again. Part 1 1 For example P2 . There's no period to choose anywhere else: it's the tab you're on. 2 Click in District ID or name and type a few letters of the name or ID. Your roster drops down. Click the student. 3 The line shows what the app found and which period it's adding to. Effective start is the first day on your caseload: leave it for a student who was there from the start of school, or change it for a mid-year arrival. 4 Each one on their own period tab. A student who's in two of your classes needs a little extra: see A student in two classes . Part 2 Why: a new student shows up mid-year. Add their row to your roster Sheet, then paste that one row into the app. No new CSV to download. It also works for a student already in the app: you get the same change review as an ARD change. 5 It's beside Add Student . The student goes on the tab you're on. 6 In your Google Sheet, click the student's row number (the grey number at the left) to select the whole row, and press Ctrl+C . Back in the app, click in the box and press Ctrl+V . Each field shows in its own labeled box, so you can check it and fix it before anything is added. Accommodations show the chips they'll become. 7 Scroll down. A date from your Sheet shows where it came from ( Read from 4/1/2027 in your Sheet. ). Check Start date , the first day on your caseload, then click Add to Period 2 (the button names your tab). Your fixes change only what goes into the app; your Sheet and CSV are never changed. 8 A green line names who was added and from when, and their card is on the tab. From here they're an ordinary student, however they got in. 9 Paste the row of a student you already have (say, after an ARD raised their minutes) and the box lists each difference, In the app now beside Pasted , with a tick box. Untick anything you don't want, set the Effective date , and click Save approved changes . A row that matches exactly says No changes . More in Enter an ARD change . ✓ You'll know it worked The line Added Noodle, Penelope Ann (0093310) to period 2, effective … The student's card appears on that tab. Pasting: Added Crumpet, Biscuit Lee (0099010) to period 2, effective … ✕ Avoid these The wrong tab. Open the card's gear: Move to another period , or Delete permanently if nothing is logged yet. An old roster. New students only show once the new CSV is in the Roster folder. Or paste their row instead (Part 2). Moving columns in your Sheet. Paste names each cell from your CSV's headings, in order. Changed the Sheet's columns? Download the CSV again first. Pasting two rows. One student at a time. ⚠ If you see this \"…\" is not in the roster. Nothing was created. Check the ID, or download your roster again (the Your roster lesson). 5527140 (Pickles, Dill) is already enrolled in period 5 — showing what changed on the roster. They're already added, in the period it names. The table shows anything your Sheet changed; untick what you don't want. \"…\" is on 2 rows of your roster file (rows 5 and 18). Delete the extra row in your Sheet and download it again. 2 rows were pasted. Paste one student at a time… Click just one row number in your Sheet and copy again. Not added. The pasted row has … columns, but your roster CSV has … The message lists the CSV's headings, numbered. Your Sheet's columns have changed: download the CSV again (the Your roster lesson). I did this. Ticks this stop off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/ard.html", "title": "Enter an ARD change", "heads": ["Minutes or service type: the pencil after min/week", "Change it, set the ARD date, Save", "A green line confirms it", "Several changes at once? Paste the row from your Sheet", "The next ARD date: the pencil next to Next ARD"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson When the IEP changes ⏱ About 4 minutes 🔁 After each ARD You need: the ARD date Why: minutes, service type, accommodations, and the goal are dated: weeks before the ARD keep the old values, weeks from it on use the new ones, even weeks you've already logged. There are two ways in: the card's pencil, or pasting the student's updated row from your Sheet. 1 On the card, click the small pencil next to the weekly minutes. 2 Type the new Weekly minutes , or click ICS , RES , ICS/BIP , or SF for the service type. Set Effective date to the ARD date, then Save . 3 The card shows the new minutes from that date on. 4 Update the student's row in your Sheet first. Then on their period tab click Paste from Sheets… , copy the row in the Sheet (click its row number, Ctrl+C), and paste it in the box (Ctrl+V). The app lists each difference with a tick box. Untick anything you don't want, set the Effective date to the ARD date, and click Save approved changes . 5 Click it, pick the new date (it must be after today), and Save . ✓ You'll know it worked A green line on the card, or 2 changes approved, 0 discarded. The card shows the new values on and after the ARD date. ✕ Avoid these Dating it today instead of the ARD date. The minutes owed between the ARD and today would be wrong. Forgetting to re-paste. Weeks you already pasted into the real log before a backdated change: copy them again ( Paste a week ). A date before the student's latest change. Refused, with the earliest date you can use. ⚠ If you see this There's already a roster change for this student starting … Use that date or later. Not saved: the Next ARD date must be after today … Pick the date of the next annual ARD meeting. Nothing changed, so nothing was saved. The values you typed are what the app already has. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/august.html", "title": "August year-end", "heads": ["Back up to Google Drive first", "Settings → Danger zone → Show what this deletes…", "Type CLEAR, click Clear for the new year, and confirm", "Set up the new year"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Keep it safe ⏱ About 10 minutes, plus re-adding students 🔁 Once a year You need: next year's empty service log and roster Why: a new school year starts empty: no students, no calendar. Your settings, bell schedule, subject labels, and imported lessons stay. Re-adding students one at a time is deliberate: it catches anyone who left but was never removed. 1 Do Back up to Drive . That copy is last year, kept. 2 It states the real numbers: students, sessions, codes, and goal tracking. 3 A backup is taken first, automatically. Under the finished message, a note counts the files in your backup folder and names the …_pre-clear.db file just saved: it holds the whole year, so drag it to Google Drive. Once your logs are turned in you can delete older backups in File Explorer ( Open backup folder ); the app never deletes them itself. 4 Load the calendar from the new year's empty service log, check the bell schedule , put the new roster in the Roster folder, and add your students . ✓ You'll know it worked Cleared for the new year. A snapshot of the old year was taken first. The period tabs are empty; Settings, lessons, and units are still there. ✕ Avoid these Skipping the Drive copy. It's your record of last year. Doing it before the year is really over. Paste your last weeks into the service logs first. ⚠ If you see this The year-end clear did NOT run and nothing was deleted. Nothing was lost. Try again, or ask for help with the reason it gives. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/backup.html", "title": "Back up to Google Drive", "heads": ["When the backup panel appears, click Save a copy to Desktop", "Drag that file into Google Drive", "Tick “I moved this file into Google Drive.”", "Any time you like: Settings → Snapshots and restore"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Start here · Stop 1 of 6 ⏱ About 3 minutes 🔁 Once a week You need: Google Drive Stop 1 of 6 Why: the app saves its own backups on your computer, but those don't help if the laptop dies or is stolen. A copy in your Google Drive does. The app can't reach the internet, so it makes the copy and you carry it to Drive. Once a week, a panel reminds you, and it stays until you've done it. 1 It appears the first time you open the app, then whenever it's been 7 days since your last copy. A file named like SpEd Log Pro export 2026-10-13_1837.db lands on your Desktop. 2 Open drive.google.com , open (or make) a folder such as SpEd Log Pro backups , and drag the file from your Desktop onto it. Drive can't open the file, and that's fine: it's a backup, not a document. 3 Only once it's really in Drive. The panel goes away. 4 Save a copy to Desktop makes a copy whenever you want one, for example before a big change. The line above it says when you last did it. ✓ You'll know it worked The panel is gone. Settings → Snapshots and restore says Last saved to Desktop: … (today). The file is in your Drive folder. ✕ Avoid these Ticking the box before the file is in Drive. The tick is your promise; the app can't check it. Leaving the copies on the Desktop only. They protect nothing until they're in Drive. ⚠ If you see this An error message and a Back to the app button The Desktop copy couldn't be written (a missing or blocked Desktop folder). You're not locked out; the panel comes back later. If it keeps happening, ask your tech support about the Desktop folder. I did this. Ticks this stop off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/bells.html", "title": "Set your bell schedule", "heads": ["Settings → Bell schedule", "Type each period’s start and end time", "Click Save bell schedule", "See it at work: Settings → Period surplus report"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Start here · Stop 3 of 6 ⏱ About 3 minutes 🔁 Once a year You need: your period times Stop 3 of 6 Why: each period's length is the most minutes the app will credit a student for one day in that period, it's the room time behind each card's surplus number, and it's how much a student's absence lowers the minutes owed that day. The times on a new install are placeholders (50 minutes each), so set yours. 1 Click Settings , then the Bell schedule title to open it. 2 Click the hour, type it, then the minutes, then AM or PM. The Length column updates as you type, so you can check each period comes out right. 3 The line under it says Saved. 4 Pick Per student or Per class (period) , the dates, then Compute . Each week shows Room time (the period's length for every day the student was present), Adjusted (what was owed), Period surplus , and Notes explaining the week in words: each coded day and what it did to Adjusted, then how room time was counted, like Present 2 days × 110 minutes = 220 room time. Print… beside Compute prints it or saves a PDF. ✓ You'll know it worked Saved. under the button. Each period's Length matches your real bell schedule. ✕ Avoid these Leaving the placeholders. Fifty-minute periods that aren't real give wrong surplus numbers. Mixing up AM and PM. An end time before the start time is refused. ⚠ If you see this A red message after Save A period is missing a time, or ends before it starts. Fix that row and save again. I did this. Ticks this stop off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/builder.html", "title": "Set up the Leveled Guide Builder", "heads": ["Get a prompt: quick, or made for your class", "In Claude, make a new project", "Paste the prompt into the project's instructions", "Fill in your course, grade, and large print", "Paste your course's TEKS at the very bottom", "Use it: upload a lesson"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Lessons & leveled guides ⏱ About 10 minutes 🔁 Once You need: a Claude account and your course's TEKS Why: the Leveled Guide Builder is a Claude project that turns one lesson into three leveled guides, a large-print set, a vocabulary sheet, a teacher guide, and a TEKS report, plus the two files SpEd Log Pro imports so it can fill in the TEKS and suggest each student's accommodations. 1 The prompt is the set of instructions that turns Claude into the Leveled Guide Builder. Pick one: 🛠 Custom: the Prompt Maker Recommended for other subjects About 10 minutes · Claude asks you questions with choices, then writes a prompt fitted to your subject, curriculum, and lessons 📋 Copy the Prompt Maker Download as text In Claude, start a new chat (an ordinary one, not inside a project). Paste the Prompt Maker, attach one typical lesson or assignment (no student names), and send. Answer its questions. Each one gives choices and marks the one it recommends; pick one or type your own. When it writes your prompt, copy the whole block. That's the prompt you paste in step 3, with your course, grade, and large-print choice already filled in. You still paste your TEKS (step 5). Copy buttons copy the whole thing, clean. Copying out of the PDF can break the lines oddly; the PDF is for reading or printing. Made your builder before version 0.4.21? Its files still import. To get Look for lines on your goal alerts, update its instructions: copy the new prompt, fill in your course, grade, and large-print lines again, and paste your TEKS back at the bottom. If you made yours with the Prompt Maker, run the new Prompt Maker again. 2 Go to claude.ai and sign in. Click Projects in the left sidebar, then Create project (it may say New project ). Name it for the course, like Leveled Guide Builder (English 3) , and create it. Teach more than one subject? Make one project per course, each with its own course line and its own TEKS (steps 3 to 5): Leveled Guide Builder (English 3) , Leveled Guide Builder (Biology) . Upload each lesson in the project for its course. 3 Inside the project, find Instructions (it may say Set project instructions ) and click it. Paste the whole prompt and save. Project instructions apply to every chat you start in this project, so you do this once. 4 At the top of what you pasted, under Course setup , replace the three brackets. (A Prompt Maker prompt has these filled in already: skip to step 5.) Course: [type your course here, …] → Course: Biology Grade level: [type the grade here, …] → Grade level: 9th grade Large print: [type yes or no] → Large print: no Large print is optional. Yes makes a large-print Level 2 guide and the reading pages enlarged. No skips both, and the Guide Supports file leaves out Level 2 Large Print; SpEd Log Pro simply never uses its L2 LP button. 5 The last line says [Paste the complete, current TEKS for your course here …] . Replace that line with your course's TEKS: the course introduction and every knowledge-and-skills statement with its student expectations. You'll find them on the Texas Education Agency's TEKS pages (Texas Administrative Code, Chapters 110 to 130). The builder cites codes only from what you paste here, so paste the whole course, and update it when the TEKS change. Save the instructions. 6 Start a new chat inside the project and add the lesson document (drag it in, or use the paperclip or + ). The builder starts right away and makes ten files (eight without large print): the three leveled guides, large print if you chose it, vocabulary, a teacher guide, a TEKS report, and two CSVs. Download them all. Keep … - TEKS Alignment.csv and … - Guide Supports.csv in the same folder, then follow Leveled guides: set up to bring them into SpEd Log Pro. Look for. The TEKS report gives each expectation a Look for : one sentence saying where on the handout to check it and what to count, like Questions 2 and 4: check that each names a cause and the effect it led to. The TEKS Alignment CSV carries it in a sixth column, Look for . When a goal alert comes from that activity, the app shows it in a black box on the Record progress pop-up (see Record goal progress , step 3). ✓ You'll know it worked The first reply lists the lesson title, the reading pages, the handout pages, and the key words, then the files follow. Ten files (eight without large print), named with the lesson title, including … - TEKS Alignment.csv and … - Guide Supports.csv . ✕ Avoid these Changing the support names in Step 8. SpEd Log Pro refuses a Guide Supports file with any other name. Uploading anything with student names. The builder only needs the lesson. Chatting outside the project. A chat outside it doesn't have the instructions. ⚠ If you see this It cites a TEKS code your course doesn't have The TEKS at the bottom are missing or from another course. Paste your course's TEKS and try again. Only some of the files Ask: \"Make the rest of the ten files.\" Or ask for one: \"Just make the Guide Supports CSV for this lesson.\" I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/calendar.html", "title": "Load this year’s calendar", "heads": ["Download an empty service log", "Settings → Calendar ingest → Choose a service log file…", "Check the preview, then Confirm and save calendar"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Start here · Stop 2 of 6 ⏱ About 5 minutes 🔁 Once a year You need: an empty service log Stop 2 of 6 Why: the app builds its school calendar from an empty copy of the service log: which days are school days, and which row each date sits on, so it can tell you exactly which cell to paste into. It only reads the dates; it never changes the real log. 1 Open a blank service log workbook in Google Sheets: the district's empty template, or a fresh copy with no student filled in. Choose File → Download → Microsoft Excel (.xlsx) . The dates are the same in every log, so an empty one works and keeps student information off your computer. 2 Pick the .xlsx you just downloaded (it's in your Downloads folder). 3 It names the tab it read (ELA, Math, SS, or SC), how many dates (about 167), and the four nine-weeks with their first cells. A yellow note about a merged cell just means: watch that cell when you paste. Then click Confirm and save calendar . ✓ You'll know it worked The line Saved: 167 dates from the \"ELA\" tab. The Entry tab shows the School year . On a school day, the not a school day tag next to the date is gone. ✕ Avoid these A workbook with no ELA, Math, SS, or SC tab. The app reads the first of those it finds. Typing over dates in the file. Use the workbook as it is. ⚠ If you see this ELA tab, row 88: 2026-11-14 is a Saturday. … Fix the file and upload again. Something in the date column is off. Nothing was saved; fix that row in the workbook, download it again, and retry. None of the expected tabs (ELA, Math, SS, SC) were found. Download a real service log workbook, not a different sheet. I did this. Ticks this stop off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/catchup.html", "title": "Catch up on missed days", "heads": ["On the Entry tab, click the Unresolved dates number", "Click a line", "Fill in the day and Save", "Done? Set the Log date back to today"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Every day ⏱ About 3 minutes 🔁 Whenever the number isn't 0 You need: nothing Why: the service log can't be copied while any day in it is empty. The Entry tab counts every school day, through today, that still has nothing saved for a student, and takes you straight to each one. 1 It opens the list: one line per student per day, with the period. 2 The app sets the Log date to that day, opens that period, and outlines the student's card in blue for a few seconds. The click itself saves nothing. 3 The activity and accommodations if they were there, or an absence code if they weren't. Several students missing the same day? Type the class activity at the top once and Save: they all clear together. 4 While you've picked a date yourself, the app leaves it alone, so put it back when you've caught up. The From and To boxes narrow the list to a range; Whole year resets it. ✓ You'll know it worked Back on Entry, the number has dropped. With nothing left: Nothing unresolved in this range. ✕ Avoid these Logging today's class on a past date. Check the Log date before you start typing. A long list for a new student. Their start date may be too early: they owe days from that date on. ⚠ If you see this The Log date isn't today Normal while catching up. Change it back when you're done. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/first-day.html", "title": "Log your first day", "heads": ["Type the class activity at the top", "Mark the accommodations each student used", "The Save button turns yellow: click it", "Green means saved"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Start here · Stop 6 of 6 ⏱ About 5 minutes per class 🔁 Every class, every day You need: your students added Stop 6 of 6 Why: this is the daily routine: the activity, the accommodations used, any absences, then Save. Nothing is saved until you click Save, and the button's colour always tells you where you stand. 1 In Activity / lesson topic for everyone… . It fills into every student's own Activity box; you can still change any one student's. 2 Click a chip once for ✓ delivered , twice for ✕ declined (red, crossed out), three times to clear it. Every chip starts unmarked each day. A student who wasn't there: pick AB , O , or S in Absence ; the card greys out except Individual notes… (see Absences & whole-class codes ). Notes go in the Individual notes… box. The badge right after each student's name says PRESENT , or the code you picked. 3 Unsaved changes means you have something waiting. 4 The button reads Saved , and a line says what was written. Then do the next class. ✓ You'll know it worked The button is green: Saved . A line like Saved 2 students for period 1, 2026-10-19. 0 marked absent. The Entry tab's Unresolved dates number goes down. ✕ Avoid these Leaving with yellow showing. The app asks: Save, Discard, or Cancel. Pick Save. Clearing a box to \"undo\" it. To correct saved text, type the corrected text and Save again. ⚠ If you see this … isn't a school day in your calendar. Save anyway? Check the Log date at the top. If it's wrong, click Cancel and change it. Period 1 has no Activity logged …, though students were saved. Go back and add the Activity, unless there really wasn't one. I did this. Ticks this stop off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/goal-project.html", "title": "Goal Setup project: make it, then import", "heads": ["Make the project (once per course)", "Get the prompt", "In Claude, make a project for your course", "Paste the prompt into the project's instructions", "Fill in your course and add your TEKS, once", "Use it: paste a goal, pick how to track it, save the file", "Import a goal setup (once per goal)", "Click the gear, then Import goal setup…", "Check the filled-in pop-up, then Save goal", "Already tracking a goal? It's a revision", "If the file has a problem", "Both Checklist and Trial fraction on? Turn one off"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Goals ⏱ About 5 minutes to make, 3 per goal to import 🔁 Once per course; once per goal You need: a Claude account and your course's TEKS Why: a Goal Setup project is a Claude project that already holds your course's TEKS. Paste a goal's wording and it gives back the matching TEKS codes, offers ways to track the goal (a checklist or a trial fraction, with rubric levels if they fit) and lets you pick, writes the checklist items or levels and a Goal check, and makes a … - Goal Setup.csv file that fills in the goal pop-up for you. Part 1 1 This set of instructions turns a Claude project into a Goal Setup project. Your course's TEKS go in once, so after that you only paste a goal. Already made a Goal Setup project or a TEKS Finder? Keep the project and its TEKS. In its instructions, replace everything above the line TEKS reference with this prompt, and save. The new prompt asks how to track each goal before it writes the file, and adds a Goal check. It still answers with the codes first. 2 Go to claude.ai , click Projects in the left sidebar, then Create project (it may say New project ). Name it for the course, like Goal Setup (English 3) . Teach more than one subject? Make one per course, since each has its own TEKS. 3 Inside the project, open Instructions (it may say Set project instructions ), paste the whole prompt, and save. Choose Sonnet as the model: choosing methods and writing the file exactly right is where smaller models slip. 4 Change Course: [type your course here, …] to your course. Then replace the last line, [Paste the complete, current TEKS for your course here …] , with your course's TEKS from the Texas Education Agency's TEKS pages (the course introduction and every knowledge-and-skills statement). Or upload them to the project as a file and delete that line. Save. If anything is missing later, the project asks for it before it makes a file. 5 Start a chat inside the project and paste one goal's wording, with the name replaced by \"Student\" , worded the way you want it on the card. The answer starts with the codes, like 28A; 28B , then why each fits, then asks How should this goal be tracked? with 2 to 4 lettered choices, one marked (recommended) . Each choice is one scored method, a Trial fraction or a Checklist (never both), with Rubric levels added when the goal names prompting or independence. Under the choices it shows the mastery note, a Goal check (how to check the goal in class), the target ( Annual ARD unless the goal gives a date), and how the percentage counts. Answer with a letter, plus any changes (\"B, but make the goal check 3 questions\"). Then it writes the file, with a Goal check row the app fills in for you. Save the file (if it only shows the text in a box, copy it into Notepad and save it ending in .csv ). Then import it (Part 2) from the student's gear. On a version before 0.4.20? There's no import yet: copy the first line of codes into TEKS code(s) when you set up goal tracking . ⚠ Never paste a student's name, ID, birthday, or anything else that identifies them. Copy only the goal's wording, and replace the name with \"Student\" first. If a name slips in, the project won't repeat it or put it in the file, and will remind you. Part 2 Why: Importing the project's file fills in the goal pop-up for you, so you check it instead of typing it. Nothing is saved until you click Save goal . 6 On the student's card, click the gear . In the pop-up, click Import goal setup… and choose the file your project made. The gear on that student's card tells the app whose goal it is, so the file never needs a name. 7 Set up goal tracking opens with the file's goal text, mastery note, Goal check, TEKS, and tracking methods. A grey line at the top says which file filled it. The file never sets Effective or Next ARD , so set those, and Target if the file didn't. Change anything you like, then Save goal , the same as if you'd typed it. The file's goal text becomes the card's IEP Goal wording when you save. Paste the goal into your project worded the way you want it on the card. 8 If the student already has a tracked goal, Revise goal opens instead, with a second grey line naming what the file changes, like Changed from the current goal: Goal text, TEKS, Checklist items. The old goal's progress stays. When the file's goal text differs from the current goal's, This is a new goal (start a new count) at the bottom starts ticked: leave it ticked for a new goal, or untick it if the file only corrects the wording. See Goal progress , step 7. 9 Nothing is filled in. The gear pop-up lists every problem by line number, like a second goal in one file, a name line, a method with no items, or a TEKS code it can't read. Ask your project for a corrected file (or fix it in Notepad), click Back , and import again. 10 A goal is scored one way, never both. A file that turns on both still fills the pop-up, with a yellow line, and Save goal is refused until you click one of the two off. The project prompt on this site never writes both; an older file might. ✓ You'll know it worked The answer's first line is only codes, like 28A; 28B . It asks how to track the goal before it makes a file. You get a file named like Inference questions - Goal Setup.csv . The pop-up opens filled in, with Filled from … at the top. After Save goal , a green IEP Goal and Tracking since … . ✕ Avoid these Pasting names or student details. The goal's wording is all it needs. Taking it all on trust. Check the codes and methods before you save the goal. Chatting outside the project. A chat outside it doesn't have your TEKS. Saving without checking. The project suggests; you decide. Importing on the wrong card. The gear you click decides whose goal it is. Pasting names into the project. The goal's wording is all it needs. ⚠ If you see this Paste or upload this course's TEKS first. Step 4 isn't done yet. What counts as mastery for this goal? The goal doesn't say how it's measured. Answer, and it goes on. How should this goal be tracked? Pick a letter. It waits for your answer before it makes the file. A target date is required when the target type is a fixed date. The file didn't say. Pick a Target date, or switch Target to Annual ARD. Nothing was saved: nothing changed from the goal already in effect. The file matches the goal you're already tracking. Click Cancel. Checklist and Trial fraction are both on. Turn one off before saving. Do step 10. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/goal-setup.html", "title": "Set up goal tracking", "heads": ["Click the IEP Goal line, then Set up goal tracking", "Fill in the goal", "Pick how you'll measure it", "Tracking has started"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Goals ⏱ About 6 minutes 🔁 Once per goal, revised after an ARD You need: the IEP goal and its mastery criteria Why: the app tracks one academic goal per student. You decide how you'll measure it; the app reminds you when a lesson touches it and keeps the evidence and running percentages. It never decides mastery for you. 1 On the student's card, click IEP Goal . The Goal tracking pop-up opens; click Set up goal tracking . 2 The goal text comes from your roster. Add a Mastery note in plain words (required). TEKS code(s) : the codes whose lessons count as evidence, separated by ; or , ; leave it blank if you'll measure by observation. Target : Annual ARD , where it starts (it shows the student's Next ARD date), or a fixed date. Effective is when tracking starts. Percentage counts as : how the percentages are worked out (see below). Goal check (optional) : a sentence or two on how you'll check this goal in class, for example Ask 2 to 3 questions out loud on the day's skill; record correct of asked. It shows on every Record progress pop-up, so each check is about the same size. How to write a TEKS code: use the short form, the knowledge-and-skills number plus the letter, with nothing else. Full code Type 113.41(d)(28)(B) 28B 111.39(c)(5)(A) 5A two codes 28A; 28B It's the same form the TEKS box on a period page uses and the lesson picker fills in, and a code has to match exactly to raise an alert: 28 does not match 28B . Capitals and spaces don't matter. Not sure which TEKS fit a goal? Ask an AI chat such as Claude. Paste the goal text and your course's TEKS, and ask: \"Which student expectations in these TEKS does this IEP goal practice? Give the short codes, like 28B.\" Check what it suggests before you type it in. Tired of pasting the TEKS every time? Make a project that keeps them and fills this pop-up in for you: Set up a Goal Setup project , then import its file . Writing a brand-new goal from a PLAAFP? See Write a measurable goal : that project writes the goal and its Goal Setup file together. ⚠ Never paste a student's name, ID, birthday, or anything else that identifies them. Copy only the goal's wording, and replace the name with \"the student\" first. Percentage counts as: pick one. It decides how the card's Last 5 and All-time percentages are worked out from your checklist and trial-fraction entries. Example: one day 9 of 10 (90%), another day 2 of 2 (100%). Choice How it counts The example Total checked over total possible Adds up everything checked or correct, over everything possible. A bigger entry counts more. 11 of 12 = 92% Plain average of each entry's percent Averages each entry's own percent. Every entry counts the same, however big. (90% + 100%) ÷ 2 = 95% Total over possible suits a goal measured in items or trials (\"80% of problems\"). Plain average suits a goal measured per session (\"80% in 4 of 5 opportunities\"). Rubric picks and notes never count toward either. You can change it on a revision; the newest setting works out every entry again. 3 A goal is scored one way: a Checklist of criteria (add items with + Add item , or click a starter idea) or a Trial fraction (correct of total), never both. Clicking one while the other is on turns the other off, and a grey line says so. Rubric levels can sit beside either one, because a rubric never counts toward the percentage. A typed note is always available too. Then Save goal . A goal saved before this rule with both on keeps working as it was. The next time you open Revise goal… on it, a yellow line asks you to turn one off, and Save goal is refused until you do. 4 The pop-up shows what's being tracked. On the card, the IEP Goal title turns green. ✓ You'll know it worked Tracking since … with the goal, mastery note, TEKS, and target. A green IEP Goal on the card. ✕ Avoid these Codes run together. 28a.29b reads as one code: separate them with ; or , (a yellow warning appears). Expecting a mastery verdict. The app shows the evidence; you decide. ⚠ If you see this A mastery note is required — plain words describing what counts as mastery. Fill in the Mastery note. Not saved: the Next ARD date must be after today … Enter the date of the next annual ARD meeting. Nothing was saved: nothing changed from the goal already in effect. Change something, or Cancel. Checklist and Trial fraction are both on. Turn one off before saving. Click the one you don't want. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/goal-writer.html", "title": "Write a measurable goal", "heads": ["Get the prompt", "In Claude, make a project and paste the prompt", "Add your courses' TEKS (optional, but better)", "Paste the PLAAFP and answer its questions", "Pick one of the three goals", "Read the seven parts it gives you", "Save the Goal Setup file and import it"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Goals ⏱ About 5 minutes to set up, then a few minutes per goal 🔁 Before an ARD You need: a Claude account and the student's PLAAFP Why: a goal the app can track needs one way of scoring it, a clear count, and a plan for checking it in class. A PLAAFP Goal Writer project reads the PLAAFP, asks you a few quick questions, writes three goal choices, and then gives you the finished goal with everything around it: the TEKS it anchors to, how to measure it without the app, a note for the general education teacher, a data sheet, and a … - Goal Setup.csv file that fills in the goal pop-up . It works for any course and grade. 1 This set of instructions turns a Claude project into a PLAAFP Goal Writer. There's nothing to fill in. 2 Go to claude.ai , click Projects , then Create project (it may say New project ). Name it PLAAFP Goal Writer . One project covers every course: it reads the course and grade from the PLAAFP. Open Instructions (it may say Set project instructions ), paste the whole prompt, and save. Choose Sonnet as the model. 3 Upload the TEKS for the courses you write goals for as project files (the Texas Education Agency's TEKS pages: the course introduction and every knowledge-and-skills statement). Then the codes it gives come from them. Without them it still writes the goal, but labels its codes \"Check these codes against the current TEKS before using them in the tracker.\" 4 Start a chat inside the project and paste the PLAAFP (or your notes) with the student's name removed. Add the ARD date and the date the goal takes effect if you have them. It asks only what the PLAAFP doesn't answer, one question at a time, each with lettered choices and one marked (recommended) : how progress is counted, how often, what mastery looks like, which two accommodations to name, and who collects the data. Answer with a letter. ⚠ Never paste a student's name, ID, birthday, or anything else that identifies them. The goal comes back with [Student] where the name goes; you add the name in your IEP software. If a name slips in, the project won't repeat it, and will remind you. 5 It writes three goal choices, each measured a different way (for example one correct-out-of-total, one checklist, one rubric), with a line on when each fits best. Answer A , B , or C , or say what to change. Every goal is scored one way: correct out of total or a checklist, never both. 6 Part What it is 1. The Goal The finished goal, starting In 36 instructional weeks, [Student] will… (tell it if your district uses another format) 2. TEKS Anchor The year-long strand, and Codes: … in the app's short form 3. Measuring it without the tracker The data source, how often, who collects it, how to record it 4. SpEd Log Pro setup Mastery note, Goal check, TEKS, target, method, percentage, and the Goal Setup CSV 5. Teacher Handoff A short note for the general education teacher 6. Data Sheet A table to print, one row per data point 7. Form Questions Questions for a Google Form that matches the data sheet 7 Save part 4's file (if it only shows the text in a box, copy it into Notepad and save it ending in .csv ). After the ARD, import it from the student's gear. Before you click Save goal , change [Student] in the goal text to the wording you want on the card. If the codes carried the \"check these\" label, the file leaves TEKS out: check them, then type them into TEKS code(s) . ✓ You'll know it worked Questions come one at a time, each with lettered choices. The last answer has all seven parts, and a file named like Linear equations - Goal Setup.csv . ✕ Avoid these Pasting names or student details. The PLAAFP's wording is all it needs. Taking it all on trust. It drafts; you and the ARD committee decide the goal. Chatting outside the project. A chat outside it doesn't have the instructions or your TEKS. ⚠ If you see this Check these codes against the current TEKS before using them in the tracker. There are no TEKS files in the project (step 3). Check the codes before you type them in. Which one should I finish? A, B, or C Pick one, or say what to change. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/guides-setup.html", "title": "Leveled guides", "heads": ["Set up (once per lesson)", "Keep both files from the builder in one folder", "Settings → Lesson activities → Import lesson CSV…, and pick the TEKS Alignment file", "Optional: Settings → Leveled guide supports → Supports to suggest", "Optional: fix a wording the app matches wrong", "Log a guide day", "Pick the activity with Lesson activity…", "Tap the level you handed each student", "Click the chips you delivered, then Save", "Column D shows the level"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Lessons & leveled guides ⏱ About 4 minutes to set up, 3 per guide day 🔁 Once per lesson; each day you hand out guides You need: the lesson's TEKS Alignment and Guide Supports files, in one folder Why: your Leveled Guide Builder also writes a Guide Supports file: which supports (word bank, sentence stems, chunking…) it built into each level of the guide. Import it with the lesson, and on a guide day the app shows which of each student's accommodations that guide covers. Part 1 1 No builder yet? See Set up the Leveled Guide Builder . The builder makes … - TEKS Alignment.csv and … - Guide Supports.csv . Save them together (your Downloads folder is fine). Their names must differ only at the end: if your browser saved … - Guide Supports (1).csv , take the (1) off. 2 Pick the TEKS Alignment file, not the Guide Supports file. The app finds the Guide Supports file beside it by itself. Under the activities, Guide supports lists what each level's guide has, one line each with a count; hover a line that ends in … to see it all. Tick your periods and click Import (or Replace \"…\" for a lesson you already had: its ticks carry over and no saved day changes). 3 Nothing here has to be set up: the app reads key phrases in each accommodation (\"chunks\", \"reduced length of assignments\", \"sentence stems\") to know which support covers it. Untick a support to stop its suggestions, or tick one of the four (optional) ones if your guides use it (for example fill-in-the-blank notes), then Save supports . 4 Only if you see a wrong dot, or a dot you expected is missing. Open Wordings the app matched (or with no match ), change that wording's support or mark ( No stops it), and click Save . The row says your change ; Use built-in match puts it back. Part 2 Why: tap the guide level you handed each student, and the accommodations that guide covers get a gold dot, so the ones you delivered through the guide don't get missed. Column D of the service log notes which level they had. 5 Every present student's card gets a Guide given today row for that lesson: None · L1 · L2 · L3 · L2 LP , with nothing selected. Two activities from the same lesson share one row. Absent and coded students get no row. 6 The chips that level's guide covers get a gold dot ● (built in) or a gold ? (might be: look before you click). A line under the chips names the guide. 7 A dot never marks a chip. Click each one you delivered, exactly as always, then click Save . Changing only a level makes the button read Unsaved changes . Saved before tapping a level? The row stays: tap it and Save again. Nothing carries to the next day: every row starts blank. 8 In the service log, that day's Accom / Mods ends with the level: … Verbal and visual directions (Lvl 1) . With no chip marked it reads (Lvl 1) by itself, so the log still shows a guide was given. ✓ You'll know it worked The preview lists Guide supports by level before you import. The line after importing ends Guide supports: 22 saved. Dots appear as soon as you tap a level. Column D ends with (Lvl 1) , (Lvl 3, Lvl 2) for two lessons, or (Lvl 2 LP) for large print. ✕ Avoid these Picking the Guide Supports file in Import. Pick the TEKS Alignment file; the supports come with it. Files in different folders. The app only looks beside the TEKS Alignment file. Trusting the dot. It is a reminder, not a mark. Click the chips you really delivered. Typing the activity. The row only comes from Lesson activity… , from a lesson with its Guide Supports file. ⚠ If you see this The first line must be exactly Lesson,Activity,TEKS,Percent,Expectation (or the same with ,Look for at the end) — this file's first line is Lesson,Level,Support You picked the Guide Supports file. Import again and pick the TEKS Alignment file. Guide supports: none… The app found no Guide Supports file beside it. Check it's in the same folder and named exactly like the TEKS file, ending - Guide Supports.csv . In \"… - Guide Supports.csv\": \"…\" isn't one of the guide support names The builder misspelled a support on that line. Fix the line (or ask the builder to remake the file) and import again. Whole-class code is on, so there's nothing to log. That period has a whole-class code today. Switch it off first to log the day. No Guide given today row after picking That lesson was imported without its Guide Supports file. Import it again with the file beside it (Part 1 above). A dot on the wrong chip Settings → Leveled guide supports → Fix a wording. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/import-lesson.html", "title": "Import a lesson, organize it by unit", "heads": ["Import a lesson and pick its activities", "Settings → Lesson activities → Import lesson CSV…", "Choose its unit, tick the periods that teach it, then Import", "On the period page: Lesson activity…, then a unit", "Pick the lesson", "Click each activity you want, then Done", "The boxes fill in; check them and Save", "Organize lessons into units", "Settings → Lesson activities → Units: Add unit", "Put each lesson in its unit", "Rename or delete a unit", "New lessons go straight into a unit"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Lessons & leveled guides ⏱ About 5 minutes 🔁 Once per lesson; once per unit You need: the lesson's TEKS Alignment CSV Why: your lesson project makes a TEKS Alignment file for each lesson. Import it once, and on the period page you can pick an activity instead of typing the Activity and TEKS. Its codes raise goal alerts exactly as if you'd typed them. Don't have a lesson project yet? See Set up the Leveled Guide Builder ; it makes this file. Already using leveled guides? See Leveled guides: set up . Part 1 1 Pick the lesson's … - TEKS Alignment.csv . A preview shows each activity (brackets removed from the names) and its codes in your short form ( 113.41(d)(28)(A) becomes 28A ). A Look for column? A file from a newer builder prompt has a sixth column, Look for . The preview shows each one in grey under its code, and so does the lesson's list under Imported lessons . It tells you where to check that TEKS in the activity, and it shows on the goal alerts that activity raises. A file without the column imports exactly as before. 2 Put in unit starts on the unit of the lesson you imported last; change it if this lesson belongs elsewhere (see Part 2 ). Under Show this lesson on , tick at least one period. You can tick more later under Imported lessons. 3 Click Lesson activity… . It opens on Pick a unit : the units with a lesson for this period, newest first, and No unit last. 4 The line under the title shows where you are; click Units in it, or Back , to go back. Each topic is a small grey heading, with Learn It, Comprehend It, then Apply It. 5 Each activity shows the codes it fills in. Click one and it's added right away; the pop-up stays open and marks it ✓ Added , so you can click more, or go Back to another lesson. Click Done when you're finished. 6 Each activity is added to the Activity boxes (after a ; ) and its codes to the TEKS box. Nothing is saved until you click Save . Part 2 Why: as the year goes on, the lesson list gets long. Units group your lessons, and the newest unit (highest number) is always at the top, so the unit you're teaching now is the first thing you see in Lesson activity… . 7 Type the unit's number (1 to 99) and, if you like, its name, then click Add unit . The name is optional: with none it's just Unit 6 . The new unit shows at the top of Imported lessons , saying it has no lessons yet. 8 Every lesson has a Unit dropdown on its title bar. Choose the unit, and the lesson moves there right away. Its period ticks and activities don't change. Lessons you had before units start in No unit , at the bottom. 9 Rename opens the number and name in two boxes; change them and click Save . Delete unit asks first, then moves its lessons to No unit . No lesson or activity is ever deleted with it. 10 When you import a lesson, Put in unit starts on the unit of the lesson you imported last, so a run of lessons from one unit goes in together. See step 2 above. ✓ You'll know it worked Imported \"The New Deal - Learn It\": 3 activities, 13 codes. In Unit 5 — The New Deal. Shows on P2. Picking fills the Activity and TEKS boxes. Added Unit 6 — World War II. Saved: \"…\" is in Unit 4 — The Great Depression. Lesson activity… opens on Pick a unit , newest first. ✕ Avoid these No period ticked. Import is refused until one is. Expecting it to replace what you typed. It adds after a ; . Clicking Cancel or Done to undo a pick. They only close the pop-up; delete the text from the box instead. Writing \"Unit 3\" in the name box. The number has its own box; the name is just the topic. Looking for an empty unit on a period page. The pop-up only lists units with a lesson for that period. ⚠ If you see this Not imported: \"…\" has 2 problems. Nothing was saved. Each problem is listed by line number. Fix the file and import again. No lessons for Period … yet. Tick that period for a lesson in Settings → Lesson activities. Unit 3 already exists (The Great Depression). Nothing was saved. Each number is used once. Pick another number, or Rename the one you have. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/leaves.html", "title": "A student leaves, moves, or comes back", "heads": ["Open the card’s gear", "Changing periods: Move to another period", "Leaving your caseload: Remove from my caseload", "Read what it says, then Remove", "Done", "Coming back: Settings → Archived students → Restore…"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Students ⏱ About 4 minutes 🔁 When a schedule or placement changes You need: the date it happens Why: nothing about a student is ever deleted once you've logged them. A move keeps their old period's history; a removal keeps every saved day; bringing them back starts a new stretch and leaves the earlier one as it was. 1 A pop-up with the student's name lists what you can do. Each choice shows its step in the same pop-up, with Back . 2 Pick the new period and the date the move starts , then Move . Days before it stay in the old period. A grey line says the earliest date you can use. 3 Set Remove effective to the first day they're gone (removing effective 10/21 means 10/21 is already gone), then Confirm removal . 4 It tells you how many saved days fall on or after that date. They're kept, just no longer shown for those dates. 5 If the date is after the Log date, the card stays until then. Close leaves the pop-up. 6 Each student who left is listed with every stretch they were enrolled. Click Restore… , pick the date they're back, and Confirm restore . They return to the period they were in; to change it, restore first, then move them. ✓ You'll know it worked A move: the card shows on the new period's tab from the move date. A removal: the line Removed, effective … . A restore: the student leaves the Archived list and is back on their tab from that date. ✕ Avoid these The last day present as the removal date. It's the first day gone . Delete permanently is only for a student added by mistake with nothing ever saved. Use Remove for everyone else. A student in two classes is two records: move or remove each one. ⚠ If you see this Exit date … is on or before …, when the student's current enrollment began. Pick a date after their start date. Restore date … is before the exit date …. They can't come back before they left: pick a later date. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/minutes.html", "title": "The Minutes report", "heads": ["Settings → Minutes report → Per student", "Read each week", "A whole class at once: Per class (period)", "Print it, or save it as a PDF"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Paste & report ⏱ About 4 minutes 🔁 Whenever you want to check You need: some logged weeks Why: it shows, week by week, whether a student's minutes were met, with the reason for every adjustment written out in words, so you never have to work it out yourself. 1 Click in District ID and pick the student from the list (type part of a name). Pick From and To , then Compute . 2 One card per school week, with a badge: Compliant , Short by … , or Provisional — week not finished while a day is still unlogged. The sentence underneath says why, and a table shows each day. Here Waffles was absent Wednesday, so the 160 minutes owed drop by a full 50-minute period to 110. Each week What it means Required The weekly IEP minutes, for the days the student was enrolled Adjusted Required, minus each coded day. A student code ( AB , O , S ) takes off a full period (the bell schedule's length that day), never below 0. SUB and ARD take off that day's share. SAB and FLEX take off nothing. Credited The minutes printed in column B: spread over the days served, never more than the period's length in a day Short Credited is less than Adjusted 3 Pick the period and the range, then Compute : one table, one row per student per week. A short week is shaded, and Notes says why. Here the whole class had a staff-absent day and a flex day, which lower nothing; Ziggy was also absent Tuesday, which lowered his week by a full 110-minute period. 4 Print… sits beside Compute . It's greyed until Compute shows a result, and greys again if you change a choice (period, student, a date) until you Compute again, so the printout always matches its heading. Click it and the Windows print dialog opens: pick a printer, or Microsoft Print to PDF to save a PDF for an ARD folder. The printout is just the report, headed with its name, your choices, and when it was printed. Nothing is saved. ✓ You'll know it worked A card per week (per student), or a table with a line saying how many students, weeks, and rows it listed (per class). Print… works once the report is on screen. ✕ Avoid these Reading too much into a provisional week. It changes as you log the rest of it. SAB or FLEX for anything else. They leave the minutes owed, so the week reads short. ⚠ If you see this District ID, a start date, and an end date are all required. Fill in the missing box and Compute again. No in-session dates this week fall inside the student's enrollment window — nothing is owed. A break week, or a week the student wasn't enrolled. Nothing to do. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/paste.html", "title": "Paste a week into the service log", "heads": ["Open the student’s Service log and pick a range", "Read the cell it names, then click Copy", "In the real log, click that cell and press Ctrl+V", "Missing days? Fill them in first"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Paste & report ⏱ About 3 minutes per student 🔁 Weekly, or whenever you like You need: every day in the range logged Why: this is what the app is for. It turns your saved days into text for the real service log, lined up to the right rows, and tells you exactly which cell to paste into. 1 On the card, click Service log , then This week (the week of the Log date), Last week , This grading period , Everything logged so far , or Custom range . 2 The line above the text says where it goes: paste at B68 is the row of the first date in the range. Copy says Copied — not yet confirmed pasted. 3 Open the student's service log in Google Sheets, click B68 (column B, the row for the first date), and press Ctrl + V . Sheets spreads it across columns B to G. Column A, the dates, is never touched. Column F holds the goal progress you recorded for that day, like Correct 7 of 10 (70%) or Checklist: … (2 of 3, 67%) . Logs pasted with an older version read Trials: 7/10 ; copy that range again to get the new wording. On an absent day, column G holds the card's note, like ISS, day 1 . Drawing of your service log in Google Sheets Pancake, Waffles — ELA File Edit View Insert Format Data A B C D E F G 24 Date Time ICS / RES Accom / Mods Activities Progress 67 10/16/2026 40 ICS Extended time on assignments… Central idea quiz review Correct 5 of 5 (100%) 68 10/19/2026 40 ICS Extended time on assignments… Bees in the City: vocabulary preview Checklist: Identifies the main idea (1 of 2, 50%) 69 10/20/2026 40 ICS Extended time on assignments… Main idea warm-up 70 10/21/2026 0 AB ISS, day 1 71 10/22/2026 40 ICS DECLINED: Small group… Bees in the City reading Checklist: Identifies the main idea, Cites evidence (2 of 3, 67%); … 72 10/23/2026 40 ICS Extended time on assignments… Main idea exit ticket 4 If any day in the range has nothing saved, you get the list of those dates instead of the text. Log or code them (see Catch up on missed days ), then pick the range again. ✓ You'll know it worked Each date in column A has its own minutes in B and the code or service type in C. Your note is in column G (an absent day's too), the activity in E, the goal progress in F. ✕ Avoid these Clicking the wrong cell. Always the cell the line names: everything shifts if you start a row off. Something already in column G. Before your first paste, make sure G is empty in that student's log. Forgetting to re-copy. Changed something after pasting? Copy that range again and paste over it. ⚠ If you see this This can't be exported yet — 5 dates still need minutes or a code: Do step 4. This week is not finished. These minutes will change if you log more days. Fine to paste; copy the week again once it's done. This range crosses a nine weeks boundary, so it comes out as 2 pastes. Each block has its own cell and Copy button. Paste each one where it says. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/progress.html", "title": "Goal progress: record, report, fix", "heads": ["Record progress", "Click the number next to IEP Goal", "Pick the alert you have evidence for", "Read the Goal check and the Look for", "Fill in what happened", "Save, Save score later, Dismiss, or Close", "See it in the progress log", "A new goal starts a new count", "Write a progress report", "Open IEP Goal, then Write progress report…", "Read the reminder, then OK", "Which number? Pick one", "Check the draft, edit it, then Copy", "Fix or delete recorded progress", "Open IEP Goal and click the day's line", "Pick what to delete", "Check it, then Delete", "Read the green line, and copy the log again"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Goals ⏱ About 2 minutes per alert 🔁 Whenever an alert appears; each report period You need: a tracked goal Why: when you save a day whose TEKS match a student's goal, that student gets an alert. Recording the result builds the progress log and the percentages, and it goes into column F of the service log for that day. The same goal pop-up also drafts a progress report sentence and lets you delete a mistake. Part 1 1 That number is how many alerts are waiting. Here, saving the Bees in the City reading (TEKS 28B) raised one for Waffles, on top of two older ones. 2 Open alerts lists every open alert for the student, oldest first: the date, the TEKS that matched, and that day's activity. Click the one you want. To clear a backlog in one sitting, click Work through all (oldest first) instead. 3 Under the goal, Goal check says how you planned to check this goal in class (you type it when you set up the goal ). If the day's activity came from a lesson file with a Look for column, the black box with yellow text, LOOK FOR , says where that TEKS shows up in the activity and what to count. A typed activity shows no Look for. 4 The pop-up shows the goal's own methods. Tick each Checklist item the student met (it counts as you go), or type Correct _ of _ on a trial-fraction goal. A goal has one or the other, never both. Pick a Rubric level if the goal has one, and add a Note if you like. Until you tick something the line reads Nothing ticked (not saved) . If the student tried and showed none of the items, tick None of these yet , the last row: that records a real 0%. 5 Save records it, dated to the lesson's day, and takes you back to the list (it closes once no alerts are left). Dismiss if it doesn't apply (absent, modified work): nothing is recorded. Close leaves it waiting. Save, score later is for work you'll score after class: it saves the rubric pick and the note now and leaves the alert open. The list then reads Waiting for score ; open it again when the work comes back and fill in the score. Under Work through all , Skip goes on to the next alert without saving. 6 Open IEP Goal : each day is one line with everything recorded that day, then the activity, for example Checklist 2 of 3 (67%); Rubric: With verbal prompt; Note: … . A trial reads Correct 7 of 10 (70%) . Hover over a line to see which items were ticked. Last 5 and All-time sit on top. No alert for the day you want? Record progress… opens the same pop-up for any tracked goal, dated to the Log date. If that date already has an open alert, it opens that alert instead. Next to it, Write progress report… drafts a report sentence: see Part 2 . Recorded something by mistake? See Part 3 . 7 A new count starts only when you tick This is a new goal (start a new count) at the bottom of Revise goal… (a new IEP, say). The log then shows New goal from … where it starts, greys the old goal's entries, and the percentages count only the new goal: All-time reads Since … . Leave the box unticked for a fix or an update, even one that changes the wording (a typo, new TEKS, a corrected mastery note): the count carries on. A Goal Setup file with new goal wording ticks the box for you; untick it if the file only corrects the wording. Part 2 Why: a progress report needs a sentence like the goal, rewritten as where the student is now. The app drafts it from the goal's own wording and the percentage it has worked out, so you start from a draft instead of a blank box. Nothing about it is saved. 8 On the student's card, click IEP Goal . The button sits beside Record progress… , under the progress log. 9 The percentage in a progress report is yours to decide, so check the number against the student's work. OK goes on. Hide for 3 weeks goes on too, and keeps the reminder away for every student until the 21st day after you click it. 10 Last 5 or All-time (it reads Since … after a new goal). They're the same numbers Goal tracking shows. With no checklist or trial-fraction entries yet, the box fills straight away with __% for you to type the number into. 11 The box gets the goal's wording, changed three ways: an opening like By the end of the year, , By May 2027, , or In 36 instructional weeks, comes off; every \"will\" becomes \"can\"; and the first percentage becomes the number you picked (here 80% became 83%). Edit it however you like, click Copy , and paste it into the report. Yellow notes under the box say what to check, and never stop you: the goal counts attempts (\"4 of 5\"), so count the progress log lines yourself; the goal has more than one percentage (only the first changed); it has no percentage; or it has no \"will\" to change. Part 3 Why: a score on the wrong student, the wrong day, or a typo in a note would otherwise sit in the progress log and in column F for good. You can delete one entry, or everything for that day, and then record it again the right way. There's no edit: delete, then record again. 12 In the progress log, click the line for the day you want to fix. 13 Progress on … lists each entry from that day with its own red Delete… . To clear the whole day, click Delete all for this day… . Close changes nothing. 14 The question names exactly what will go. Delete removes it; Cancel goes back. 15 Under the progress log, a green line says what went, and the percentages update. If that day had come from an alert and you deleted its last entry, the alert opens again so you can record it properly. If the day was already pasted into the service log, copy that range again and paste over it (see Paste a week into the log ). ✓ You'll know it worked The alert is gone from the list, and the number went down. One new line for that day in the progress log, and the percentages updated. An alert saved for later reads Waiting for score . The box reads like the goal, with \"can\" for \"will\" and your number in it. Paste it in the report, and it's what you copied. Deleted 1 entry from 2026-10-08. under the log. That day's line is shorter, or gone. ✕ Avoid these Saving an empty checklist to mean 0%. Nothing ticked saves nothing. Tick None of these yet for a real 0%. Dismiss when you meant Close. Dismiss records nothing and closes the alert for good. Taking the number on trust. It's worked out from what you recorded. Check it against the work. Expecting it to be kept. Nothing here is saved. Copy it before you close. Deleting to change one number. That's fine, but record it again straight away, dated to the same day (set the Log date first). Forgetting the real log. Column F still has the old text until you copy and paste that range again. ⚠ If you see this Fill in at least one method, or tick None of these yet. Tick an item, type a score, pick a level, or write a note first. Fill in at least one method before saving. The same, on a goal with no checklist. This goal counts attempts (\"4 of 5\"). The card's percentages are averages, not counts. Count the lines in the progress log before you decide. Replace your changes with a fresh statement? You typed in the box, then picked a number. Keep my changes keeps your typing; Replace starts the draft again. The alert for … is open again. You deleted the last entry from an alert's day. Record it from the alert, or Dismiss it. This can't be undone except by restoring a whole backup. Check what's named before you click Delete. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/restore.html", "title": "Restore a backup", "heads": ["Settings → Snapshots and restore → Choose snapshot to restore…", "Read what you'd lose", "Type RESTORE, click Restore this snapshot, and confirm", "Afterwards"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Keep it safe ⏱ About 4 minutes 🔁 Only if something went wrong You need: the backup to go back to Why: the app backs itself up every day you save. If something goes badly wrong, you can put the whole database back to a backup. It's whole-database or nothing: everything goes back to that moment. 1 Pick a backup from the list's folder (or a copy you downloaded from Google Drive). The Snapshot log shows when each was taken. 2 It says how many sessions and codes were logged since that backup. Those are what a restore discards. 3 The button only works once the word is typed. You'll be asked once more. 4 Before it runs, the app writes a rescue file to your Desktop listing everything it discards, and takes a backup of what you had (so a restore can itself be undone). Then it reloads and shows the result. ✓ You'll know it worked Restored from …. Discarded 2 sessions and 0 codes across 2 students. Rescue file: … ✕ Avoid these Restoring to fix one small thing. It replaces everything; correct the one thing instead. Skipping the rescue file. Retype what it lists. ⚠ If you see this This database was created by a newer version of the app … The app on this computer is older than the one that made the database. Install the newest version, then open it again. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/roster.html", "title": "Your roster: make it, download it, drop it in", "heads": ["Make the roster Sheet (once)", "Type these 8 headings in row 1, in this order", "Make column A plain text, so ID numbers keep their zeros", "Fill in one row per student", "Download it and drop it in (every time you change the Sheet)", "Download the Sheet as a CSV", "Drag it into the Roster folder, and take the old one out", "Check it in the app"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Start here · Stop 4 of 6 ⏱ About 10 minutes the first time 🔁 2 minutes each update You need: Google Sheets Stop 4 of 6 Why: the app looks up each student's minutes, accommodations, and goal in your roster when you add them. Your roster is a Google Sheet you keep; the app reads a downloaded copy of it from one folder on your computer. It only ever reads it: it never changes your Sheet or adds anyone by itself. Part 1 1 Open a new Google Sheet and name it Roster . In row 1, type these headings across columns A to H, exactly as written and in this order . Keeping this order means Paste from Sheets works later, too: Drawing of Google Sheets Roster File Edit View Insert Format Data A B C D E F G H 1 Student ID Name: Last, First min per week Qualifying condition Accommodations Service type goal Annual ARD Date 2 0071234 Pancake, Waffles Quinn 160 SLD Extended time…; Small group testing ICS Given a grade-level text… 3/20/2027 3 4410982 Sprocket, Gizmo Alan 120 OHI Frequent breaks; Graphic organizer RES Given a writing prompt… A Student ID B Name: Last, First C min per week D Qualifying condition E Accommodations F Service type G goal H Annual ARD Date 2 Click the A at the top of column A, then Format → Number → Plain text . Do this before you type IDs, or 0071234 turns into 71234 . Drawing of Google Sheets Roster File Edit View Insert Format Data Theme Number ▸ Text Alignment Wrapping Automatic Plain text Number 1,000.12 Percent 10.12% Date 9/26/2008 3 Put all of a student's accommodations in one cell , separated by semicolons: Extended time on assignments and tests; Small group testing . Minutes are a whole number. Dates can be typed 3/20/2027 . A student in two of your classes gets two rows: see A student in two classes . Part 2 4 File → Download → Comma Separated Values (.csv) . It lands in your Downloads folder, named like Roster - Sheet1.csv . Drawing of Google Sheets Roster File Edit View Insert Format Data New Open Make a copy Download ▸ Email Print Microsoft Excel (.xlsx) OpenDocument (.ods) PDF (.pdf) Comma Separated Values (.csv) Tab Separated Values (.tsv) 5 Open File Explorer ( ⊞ Win + E ). Drag the new file from Downloads into C:\\SpEd Log Pro Roster . If Windows asks whether to replace the file with the same name, choose Replace . If an older CSV with a different name is there, delete it : the folder must hold exactly one. Drawing of File Explorer 📁 Downloads Roster - Sheet1.csv 📄 lesson plan.pdf 🖼 IMG_2041.jpg ➜ 📁 C:\\SpEd Log Pro Roster Roster - Sheet1.csv Roster - Sheet1 (old).csv 🗑 Exactly one CSV in here. 6 In SpEd Log Pro: Settings → Roster → Read roster . Each field should show one of your headings beside it, and the count should match your number of students. ✓ You'll know it worked Read roster says Read \"Roster - Sheet1.csv\": 15 student rows. On a period tab, clicking District ID or name lists your students. ✕ Avoid these Two CSVs in the folder. Delete the old one every time. Moving or adding columns. Keep the 8 in this order. If you ever change them, download the CSV again before you paste a student. Typing IDs before step 2. The leading zeros are already gone; retype them. Accommodations in separate cells. One cell, semicolons. ⚠ If you see this No CSV file found in C:\\SpEd Log Pro Roster\\. The file is still in Downloads: do step 5. More than one CSV file is in C:\\SpEd Log Pro Roster\\: … Keep only one there. Delete the old file. The roster is missing a required column: … A heading is misspelled: fix row 1 and download again. I did this. Ticks this stop off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/two-classes.html", "title": "A student in two of your classes", "heads": ["In your Sheet, make one row per class", "Download the roster into the Roster folder", "On the first class’s tab, type the shared number and pick that class’s row", "Check the start date and Confirm add", "Each class is now its own student"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Students ⏱ About 5 minutes 🔁 Once per student You need: your roster Sheet Why: the app keeps one record per student, in one period. When a student needs a separate service log for each class (your Government class in P3 and your US History class in P4, each with its own minutes), you give them one roster row per class, with a class code on the end of both the ID and the name. For anyone else, add them once, the normal way. 1 Copy the student's row so there are two. On each, add the same class code to the end of the Student ID (a hyphen, then letters, or letters and numbers) and, in brackets, to the end of the name . Give each row that class's minutes, accommodations, and goal. Capitals don't matter; spelling does. Student ID Name: Last, First min per week … 0071555 -GOV Teapot, Tester (GOV) 90 … 0071555 -USH Teapot, Tester (USH) 120 … 2 File → Download → Comma Separated Values , then replace the old CSV in C:\\SpEd Log Pro Roster (see Your roster ). 3 Here on P3 : type 0071555 in District ID or name . Both rows drop down. Pick -GOV . 4 The line names the row and the period. Then go to the other class's tab (here P4 ) and do steps 3 and 4 again, picking -USH . 5 Its own card, minutes, accommodations, goal, and Service log . Paste each one's service log into that class's tab of the real log. The pasted text never includes the ID, so the log itself is unaffected. ✓ You'll know it worked A card on each tab: Teapot, Tester (GOV) on P3, Teapot, Tester (USH) on P4. Each has its own minutes. ✕ Avoid these Changing only the name. The app tells students apart by ID, so the code goes on both. Already in the app as plain 0071555? Add the two new rows, then remove the old record with its gear button (see Leaves or comes back ). The cost: ARD changes, missed days, removals, and the August re-add happen once per record. ⚠ If you see this Not added: the Student ID \"0071556-USH\" ends in USH but the name \"Kettle, Kim (GOV)\" ends in (GOV). Make them match in the Sheet. The two codes differ. Fix the row in your Sheet, download the CSV again, and add it again. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}, {"url": "lessons/update.html", "title": "Update to a new version", "heads": ["Close SpEd Log Pro first", "Get the new Setup file onto the computer", "Double-click it. If Windows says it protected your PC: More info, then Run anyway", "Let it install. It asks no questions", "Check it worked"], "body": "🖨 Print this lesson Keep it safe ⏱ About 5 minutes 🔁 Whenever a new version comes out You need: the new Setup file Why: a new version adds features and fixes. You install it over the one you have: no uninstalling, and your students, days, goals, settings, and backups all stay exactly where they are. Windows doesn't know this app, so it shows a warning or two on the way. This lesson shows each one and what to click. 1 Save anything you're in the middle of, then close the app with the ✕ at the top right. Closing it saves a backup if you saved anything since you opened it. For extra peace of mind, make a Desktop copy before you update. 2 The file is named like SpEd Log Pro v2 Setup ….exe , ending in the version number. Use only the file whose name starts with Setup . If it's on a flash drive, drag it to the Desktop. If you download it from Google Drive, you may see one or both of these: Drawing of the download warnings Google Drive ⚠ Google Drive can't scan this file for viruses. This file is too large for Google to scan. Would you still like to download it? Download anyway ➜ Downloads ⚠ SpEd Log Pro v2 Setup ….exe isn't commonly downloaded. Make sure you trust it before you open it. Delete Keep Click Download anyway , then Keep . In Edge, Keep may be under the … next to the download, followed by Show more → Keep anyway . Both warnings appear because the file is large and not yet well known, not because anything is wrong with it. 3 A blue box may appear. It doesn't block the app; it hides the button. Click More info : the box now names the file and says Unknown publisher , and a Run anyway button appears. Click it. Drawing of Windows SmartScreen Windows protected your PC Microsoft Defender SmartScreen prevented an unrecognized app from starting. Running this app might put your PC at risk. More info Don't run ➜ Windows protected your PC App: SpEd Log Pro v2 Setup ….exe Publisher: Unknown publisher Run anyway Don't run No Run anyway button at all, or a message that your administrator blocked it? Your district has turned off installing apps on that computer. Ask your technology office to install the Setup file for you. 4 A small window shows a green bar for a few seconds. There are no choices to make and no administrator password: it installs for you only, in the same place as before. When it finishes, SpEd Log Pro opens by itself. Drawing of the installer SpEd Log Pro v2 Setup Installing, please wait… If you forgot step 1 , the installer stops with a box saying SpEd Log Pro v2 cannot be closed. Please close it manually and click Retry to continue. Close the app, then click Retry . Drawing of the \"cannot be closed\" box SpEd Log Pro v2 Setup ⚠ SpEd Log Pro v2 cannot be closed. Please close it manually and click Retry to continue. Retry Cancel 5 The version next to the app's name, at the top left, now shows the new number, and your students and saved days are all there. Some versions change how the database is stored: the first time the new version opens, it saves a pre-migrate backup and updates the database by itself. There's nothing to click, and nothing to do afterwards. Skipping versions is fine. You can go from any older version straight to the newest one; it brings the database up to date in one step. ✓ You'll know it worked The app opens by itself after installing. The version at the top left is the new number. Your students and days are all there. ✕ Avoid these Uninstalling first. There's no need, and it's one more chance for a mistake. Running the other .exe. Use the file whose name starts with Setup . Installing an older version over a newer one. See the box on the right. ⚠ If you see this SpEd Log Pro — cannot open database. This database was created by a newer version of the app… An older version was installed over a newer one. Your data is safe: install the newest Setup file again. SpEd Log Pro v2 cannot be closed. The app is still open. Close it and click Retry. I did this. Ticks this lesson off in the menu (saved in this browser only)."}];
